# Typing Pet — grant the active user read-only access to keyboard devices.
#
# GNOME Shell cannot observe global keystrokes on Wayland, so Typing Pet reads
# key events straight from /dev/input/event* — read-only, keycodes only, never
# text. This rule is what grants that access.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" lets systemd-logind grant a dynamic ACL to the user of the
# *active* session. It needs no group, no per-user configuration and no
# re-login, and the access is revoked when the session ends. systemd already
# uses this mechanism for joysticks (see /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-uaccess.rules).
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow:
#   - keyboards only (ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD) — never mice, touchpads or gamepads
#   - read-only; Typing Pet never writes to input devices
#
# NOTE ON TRADE-OFFS: the Arch Wiki advises against uaccess on input devices,
# because a user able to read them could keylog. That capability is inherent to
# this application (as it is to every Linux typing-sound tool); there is no
# Wayland API that grants "typing happened" without it. We apply the tag
# deliberately and narrowly instead of adding the user to the broad `input`
# group. Delete this file (and reload udev) to revoke access entirely.
#
# Installed by the package to /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/. Manual install:
#   sudo install -m644 70-typingpet.rules /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input

SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ENV{ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD}=="1", TAG+="uaccess"
